# That Moon! Beneath That Full Moon!
Tamil title: அற்றைத் திங்கள்! அவ்வெண்ணிலவில்!

Whenever the movement we ordinary people have formed for the liberation of the homeland issues a call, what do those who govern see?

An army rises from somewhere. Unknown people assume the aspect of warriors. The voice that calls comes only from a hut, and the heroes who answer it are not inhabitants of mansions. The moment the movement sends its announcement, a heroic roar is heard in the field. No thought seems to arise about the severity of the struggle or the consequences of attack.

If heroes respond in this way to the affectionate call of our movement, which possesses no support other than its great purpose that the homeland must be liberated, and march proudly into the field, is it not like tasting the pure honey that a liberation camp ought to receive? Is not the condition of our movement in these days like the heart of a lover walking along and thinking, "Once I cross that forest where elephants roam, there is a small stream; it is easy to cross, and beyond it—she!" Why then should joy not overflow?

Those who seized the hill must return it. We who demand it are its rightful owners. The hill is our homeland.

A beautiful small hill serves as a fortress for the kingdom.

The kingdom is small in extent but great in distinction.

There was no one who did not praise it. Poets celebrated it for its generosity and cultured conduct. The people prospered and rejoiced in life.

Great kingdoms engaged in petty acts. This kingdom shone as a beautiful realm where virtue and love were joined.

Pari's kingdom appears before the great poets as a golden portrait worthy of praise.

Pari had two daughters, maidens endowed with stately beauty and abundant learning.

Pari, free from greed for land, ruled by embracing his people and making a distinguished form of government his ideal. He fell victim to the hostility of those who ruled vast territories. The kingdom lost its beauty. With their rights taken away, Pari's daughters became golden portraits covered in dust.

The aged Avvai encountered the young women in this condition and filled with tears. The daughters of the generous Pari saw the great Tamil woman. They thought of "those days"—the days when their father and beautiful kingdom remained—not merely to sob, but from longing that those days no longer existed in which they could welcome and honour Avvai in a manner worthy of her learning.

Would not Pari's daughters possess precisely this character? The enemy could carry away only the soil. Where could character go? Who could seize it?

"That moon, beneath that full moon!" the maidens say, melting in grief.

The white moon; its cool light upon the hill; and Pari, whose character was purer than the nature of that light.

"Our father was with us, and no other had taken our kingdom. Was that not the time in which we could receive the revered mother's visit in a fitting manner?" With this thought, Pari's daughters said, "Then we possessed our father, and others had not seized our hill." But now strangers had captured their hill. How had that become possible? Was Pari not both valiant and tender-hearted? "Now we have no father. Our father has died, and the hill has gone to our enemies. That is why."

By expressing it so, they moved the heart of aged Avvai then—and, indeed, our hearts when we read it today.

Today we Dravidians stand in the same condition as Pari's daughters, who melted in grief while thinking of the joy they had lost: "That moon, that full moon, the hill, our father!"

We have lost cool radiance and self-rule; the country has become a forest of servitude. Yet just as Pari's daughters did not lose their character even after losing their incomparable kingdom and the mighty father who caused it to flourish, we too have not lost our character though we have lost self-rule. We have therefore not lost the strength or opportunity to recover our lost happiness.

It is true that this present moon brings humiliation, blame, and hardship. Even so, we hear the sound of hope declaring that the hill we lost will again be ours. New tension enters every nerve. Longing dies in the eye and sparks of enthusiasm arise. We have sworn an oath; it is not a heated word. Our kingdom, caught and ruined in the grip of malicious minds, must become ours again. We have dedicated ourselves to the rare work required for that end.

We shall strive. We shall triumph!

(*Arappor*, 31 August 1962)

---NOTES---
The title and central refrain invoke the classical lament of Pari's daughters, conventionally known from *Purananuru*. The essay uses their lost hill and father as an analogy for lost Dravidian self-rule. OCR “சிறு குண்டு” is translated as the clearly intended “சிறு குன்று,” small hill; source text remains verbatim.
